Next is Engine.js. Main design:

- constructor(container, { antialias }) — creates the renderer and appends the canvas to container.
- scene, camera (PerspectiveCamera 50, aspect ratio, near .1 far 100), position set by game.
- clock: real dt via performance.now; timescale is a property that HitStop sets every frame (engine.timescale).
- onFrame callback: engine.onFrame = (dt) => {...} — dt is already scaled. Also expose rawDt.
- resize handling with ResizeObserver or window resize listener.
- dispose(): cancel RAF, remove listeners, traverse the registry and call .dispose() on each item; renderer.dispose(); forceContextLoss? No — just dispose.

Registry: engine.track(resource) returns resource; dedup via Set.

Loop:
```js
_tick = (now) => {
  if (!this.running) return;
  this._raf = requestAnimationFrame(this._tick);
  const rawDt = Math.min((now - this._last)/1000, 0.1); this._last = now;
  const dt = rawDt * this.timescale;
  try { this.onFrame?.(dt, rawDt); } catch (e) { console.error(e); }
  this.renderer.render(this.scene, this.camera);
};
```

Camera shake is applied by the game inside onFrame. No problem.

InputController: as planned. Buttons: fire, pause, primary, secondary. Keyboard mapping:
- fire: Space, KeyJ? Classic: Space to shoot. Also KeyZ.
- pause: Escape, P (KeyP), Enter? Let's go with Escape/KeyP.
- primary: Enter (menu confirm).
- movement x axis: ArrowLeft/A = -1, ArrowRight/D = +1; also allow vertical? Not needed for Space Invaders but keep the y-axis generic (ArrowUp/W etc.) — useful for other games in the collection too.

Gamepad mapping (standard layout): axes[0] → x; buttons: 0 (A) → primary/fire, 2 (X) → fire alternative?, 9 (start) → pause. Let's do this: A(0)=primary+fire, X(2)=fire, B(1)=secondary, Start(9)=pause.

Snapshot object is reused; edge detection: track previous state per button name; expose `pressed(name)` and `released(name)`? Simpler: snapshot.edges = Set of buttons pressed this frame (computed in update). Game checks input.snapshot.edges.has('fire').

update():
```js
const s = this.snapshot;
s.axes.x = keyboard axis + gamepad axes[0] (take max absolute value)
buttons...
edges: newly true ones.
```

GlassUI: build DOM with template strings, CSS string injected once. Methods:
- mount(rootEl): create .glass-ui root inside it.
- setScreen(name|null): show/hide screens: menu, pause, gameover; hud always visible during play (toggle via class).
- hud({score,best,lives,wave,combo}) — update text nodes; combo chip pulses when >1.
- on(action, cb) — actions: 'start','restart','resume','pause','menu'. Wire buttons + keyboard? Keyboard is handled by the game calling ui.on... Actually simpler: GlassUI exposes button callbacks via .on('primary', fn), and the game also handles keyboard edges itself (e.g., Enter to start). To avoid double-triggering, let's have the game drive everything from input edges and use UI buttons only for mouse. That is, GlassUI.on(action, cb) fires on click; the game separately checks input edges for the same action. Both call the same handler — no problem (clicks don't generate keyboard events).
- toast(text): temporary message? Not needed; floating text covers it.

CSS: neon cyan #00f6ff, magenta #ff2bd6, amber #ffc857; glass panels rgba(10,14,30,.55) blur 14px border 1px rgba(0,246,255,.35); text-shadow glow.

ComposerSetup:
```js
import { EffectComposer } from 'three/addons/postprocessing/EffectComposer.js';
import { RenderPass } from 'three/addons/postprocessing/RenderPass.js';
import { UnrealBloomPass } from 'three/addons/postprocessing/UnrealBloomPass.js';
import { OutputPass } from 'three/addons/postprocessing/OutputPass.js';

export function setupComposer(renderer, scene, camera, opts = {}) {
  const composer = new EffectComposer(renderer);
  const renderPass = new RenderPass(scene, camera);
  composer.addPass(renderPass);
  const bloom = new UnrealBloomPass(new THREE.Vector2(w,h), strength, radius, threshold);
  composer.addPass(bloom);
  const output = new OutputPass();
  composer.addPass(output);
  return { composer, setBloom(strength,radius,threshold){...}, setSize(w,h){composer.setSize; bloom.setSize? bloom.resolution.set}, dispose(){ passes forEach dispose?.(); composer.dispose?.() } };
}
```
Note: UnrealBloomPass has .dispose(). RenderPass doesn't need it. EffectComposer.dispose exists in r160 (disposes render targets). Guard with optional chaining just in case.

AudioEngine: as planned. Implement carefully.

MusicSequencer: step sequencer using AudioContext time scheduling and a look-ahead timer (setInterval 25ms, schedule up to 0.12s ahead). Tracks defined by the game? Better: MusicSequencer takes a track object {bpm, steps: [ {time(step), notes:[{freq,dur,type,gain} ] } ]}. Simpler API: start(trackName) with built-in tracks 'invaders' (4-note bass + arpeggio). Implement generically:

```js
class MusicSequencer {
  constructor(audio){ this.audio = audio; this.playing=false; this.step=0; this.nextTime=0; this.timer=null; this.track=null; this.intensity=1; }
  start(track){...}
  stop(){}
  setIntensity(v) // affects gain/filter cutoff + tempo multiplier
  _schedule(){ while(nextTime < ctx.currentTime+ahead){ playStep(step, nextTime); advance by stepDur/bpm*intensity? }}
}
```

Track format: { bpm, stepsPerBar, pattern: array of length N; each entry is null or [freqOrArray, durBeats, type, gainMul] }.

Invaders track (iconic): bassline E2 B2 C#3 D3 (E2=82.41, B2=123.47, C#3=138.59, D3=146.83) one per bar; plus a 16th-note arpeggio pattern in the upper register with some rests. Define an 8-step (eighth-note) pattern:
bass: [E2,-,-,-, B2,-,-,-, C#3,-,-,-, D3,-,-,-] each held for 1 beat? The classic is one note per bar. Let's do a 4-bar loop of 16 steps (eighth notes): bass at step 0: E2 (dur 7), step 8: B2... hmm, the classic has each note lasting about 1 bar. Simplify: 32-step pattern; bass notes at 0(E2,dur 15), 16(B2? no—)

The actual classic order: E, B, C#, D — one per bar (4 bars). So a 64-step loop (4 bars × 16 steps):
- step 0: bass E2 dur ~30 steps
- step 16: bass B2? no wait, the order is E→B→C#→D. Yes: 0:E2, 16:B2... hmm, actually I think it's E, B, C#, D. Yes.
- Arpeggio: repeating 16th-note pattern of [E4, G#4? ...]. Let's make a pleasant retro arpeggio: steps where (step%4===0): notes cycling through [329.63(E4), 415.3(A4), 493.88(B4), 659.25(E5)] with some rests, square wave, low gain.

Good enough — it'll sound chiptune-ish and retro.

Intensity: tempo multiplier = lerp(0.9, 1.25, intensity); arpeggio gain scales; add a noise hat at high intensity? Keep it simple: tempo + filter cutoff on the music bus.

